<h1 class="d-flex flex-column gap-2">
  <span>What Is a Work Log and Why Bother? (a.k.a. Your Brag List)</span>
</h1>

<div>
  
</div>


<div class="gap formatted-content">
    <section style="font-family: poppins, sans-serif; padding: 2rem; max-width: 800px; margin: auto;">
  <h2 style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 1rem;">📝 What Is a Work Log and Why Bother? (a.k.a. Your Brag List)</h2>

  <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6;">
    A work log — or as we like to call it, a <strong>brag list</strong> — is a record of what you’ve been working on, learning, and improving month after month.
    Think of it as your personal growth diary. But with fewer feelings and more Git commits. 😎
  </p>

  <div style="margin-top: 1.5rem; background-color: #f0f8ff; border-left: 4px solid #028ECA; padding: 1rem;">
    <ul style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6; list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0;">
      <li>✅ A summary of what you worked on (projects, tickets, learning modules, bugs squashed)</li>
      <li>🏆 <strong>Achievements</strong> — big or small, a win is a win</li>
      <li>🔥 <strong>Failures and learnings</strong> — yes, these belong here too!</li>
      <li>🌟 <strong>Highlights of the month</strong> — memorable events, breakthroughs, or proud moments</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 1rem;">
      You can:
      <br/>— Keep it private, just for yourself or your team
      <br/>— Or share it — as a post, article, or even a short video — with your social media network or community
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6; font-style: italic; color: #333; margin-top: 1rem;">
      This isn’t about bragging in the bad way. It’s about being proud of progress. Period.
    </p>
  </div>
    <section>
    <h4 style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 1rem;">🎥 Watch: A Crucial Habit I NEVER Considered As A Developer</h4>
<iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/hcQ7avj6Zrg?si=Deyaw_vpjUEy0qNz&amp;start=182" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </section>
  <h3 style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">💡 Why Bother with a Brag List?</h3>

  <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem; background-color: #05F283; border-left: 4px solid #4CAF50; padding: 1rem;">
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6;"><strong>📘 1. You learn more when you reflect</strong><br/>
      Keeping track of what went well — or what didn’t — helps you spot patterns, avoid repeated mistakes, and double down on what works.
      You will also have a clear record of your development, making it easier to see how far you&rsquo;ve come and identify areas for further improvement.
    </p>
    <blockquote style="font-style: italic; border-left: 4px solid #ccc; padding-left: 1rem; margin: 1rem 0;">
      “You don’t learn from experience. You learn from reflecting on experience.” — John Dewey
    </blockquote>
  </div>
    <section style="font-family: poppins, sans-serif; padding: 2rem; max-width: 800px; margin: auto;">
        

 <div class="substack-post-embed"><p lang="en">Read: Habits I recommend to succeed as a developer by Tiger Abrodi</p><p>My personal take on good habits for developers.</p><a data-post-link href="https://www.saiyangrowthletter.com/p/habits-i-recommend-to-succeed-as">Read on Substack</a></div><script async src="https://substack.com/embedjs/embed.js" charset="utf-8"></script>
 </section>

  <div style="margin-top: 1.5rem; background-color: #FBD437; border-left: 4px solid #FB8C00; padding: 1rem;">
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6;"><strong>🚀 2. You’re building your software engineer brand</strong><br/>
      Tracking your progress shows consistency, growth, commitment and intentionality.
      Whether you&rsquo;re job hunting or freelancing, a well-maintained work log can help establish you as a valuable and growing professional in your field.
      <br/><em>It’s your portfolio in progress.</em>
    </p>
  </div>
